# Line endings.
#
# The deploy tars the working tree straight onto a Linux server and runs the
# shell scripts there. Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, so
# without this every .sh is checked out with CRLF and fails on the server the
# moment bash reads it:
#
#   ops/migrate.sh: line 22: $'\r': command not found
#   ops/migrate.sh: line 23: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# That is not hypothetical — it is what a deploy from a Windows checkout did,
# and it stayed hidden only because the script that ran the migrations was a
# copy that had never been through a git checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: read by tools on the server, not by anything on Windows
# that cares about CRLF.
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
